{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons","id":912624,"title":"Heretical Thoughts on Griffin (and the Red Talons)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-06-11T21:04:20-07:00","id":912624,"title":"Heretical Thoughts on Griffin (and the Red Talons)","content":"This is yet another thread where I look at a tribe and its totem, and discuss ideas on how I'd develop it a little differently based on certain facts or legends the original writers seemed to ignore. Or at least interpret certain canonical ideas and concepts differently than is usually portrayed. While I start with Griffin as a totem based on its legends, this is actually more about the Tribe and a way to allow them to be better rounded and more playable.<br /> <br /> The Red Talons are yet another tribe many people have trouble with. They seem very limited in a dead-end way, and people can't figure out what to do with them. So I think ANY new ideas would be very appreciated. By moving beyond a fairly one dimensional portrayal, we give more opportunities for PCs and more interesting depictions of NPCs.<br /> <br /> Griffon is the tribal totem of the Red Talons, and in Garou lore he is the patron of predators and protector of those animals hunted to extinction by man. As a pack totem, he won't take any children who are homids. In essence, he is the spirit of nature red in tooth and claw. But in actual myth and legend, Griffin is many other things, few of which are incorporated into the game. But real myth and game lore can be reconciled.<br /> <br /> Rather than a summary of various things, in this thread I'll be talking about different ideas in their own posts. If I try to include everything, there is just way too much for one post or even two or three. Rather than post everything at once like I usually do, I'm going to post them individually. I'll do a few now and allow people to comment before I post some of my other ideas.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-11T21:05:45-07:00","id":912625,"title":"","content":"<b>Totem of Leadership</b><br /> <br /> Because the lion was traditionally considered the king of the beasts and the eagle the king of birds, the griffin was thought to be an especially powerful and majestic creature. Therefore, the griffin was considered to be the king of all creatures \u2013 both those on land and in the air.<br /> <br /> The combination of falcon (totem of Silver Fang) and lion (totem of White Howlers) is interesting. Both tribes are known for the white fur which is strongly associated with polar conditions. In Garou history, this marks them as tribes existent during the Ice Age, and thus are likely the two eldest tribes.<br /> <br /> By adopting this totem, did the Red Talons purposefully or inadvertently challenge for leadership in the Garou Nation? Was it a not so subtle rebuke to the Silver Fangs that alternate leadership was possible? Did lupus from one or more of these tribes depart in protest on the ending of the Impergium? Or is it just a coincidence? Might Red Talons today consider themselves a potential leader for the Garou Nation because of the nature of their totem \u2013 or at least leader of the lupus Garou? Perhaps the formation of the Red Talon tribe is much older than expected, perhaps even done in opposition to the leadership role displayed early on by the Silver Fangs.<br /> <br /> This may explain why the Lion totem because part of Griffin's brood after his tribe fell to the Wyrm. It is possible that Talons envision doing same for Falcon if the Silver Fangs ever fall as well.<br /> <br /> Red Talons, as a lupus only tribe, have always been connected to the lupus of other tribes. By interpreting Griffin as a \u201croyal\u201d totem though, this could make Red Talon interactions with other totems even more interesting. Perhaps Red Talon \u201cdiplomats\u201d are in constant contact with the lupus of other tribes, particularly Silver Fangs, reminding them they too have a leadership pedigree. Red Talons won't be machiavellian manipulators or particularly intent on seizing leadership for its own sake. But they could put themselves up as a candidate should the Silver Fangs fall or lose enough prestige.<br /> <br /> Realistically, the Red Talons have no such chance among the homids. But it might just threaten a split with the lupus of the other tribes simply because of the nature of the totem (and not the merits of the Red Talons themselves).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 06-12-2016, 03:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-11T21:15:20-07:00","id":912630,"title":"","content":"<b>Guardian of the Divine</b><br /> <br /> In antiquity, Griffin was a symbol of divine power and a guardian of the divine. It is possible to reimagine its role as a sacred protector or divine punisher, as being specifically in regards to humanity \u2013 Gaia created Griffin specifically to punish man when he trespassed the natural order. At least, that is how the Red Talons would put it. Other tribes would disagree. But clearly, it would work well that Griffin\u2019s anger towards humanity is based on something essential \u2013 perhaps some mythological crime of humans against Gaia.<br /> <br /> I originally thought it would be interesting to tie it to the Prometheus myth \u2013 for bringing fire to man, Prometheus was punished by being chained to a mountain and a monstrous eagle ate his liver every day. Since the mastery of fire was a major moment in mankind\u2019s history and allowed humanity to overcome the natural limits, it has the earmarks of something Griffin would really hate. And a monstrous eagle could easily turn out to be a griffin. However, I think man's mastery of fire is just too old for the purposes of the game. I'd like it to be something that could take place in a decent timeframe.<br /> <br /> Then I thought that agriculture might be a good \u201ccrime\u201d. Griffin is a predator and heavily associated with predatory species. Primitive man was originally a hunter, and ostensibly at that time could have had some kind of relationship with Griffin. However, by choosing agriculture, man stopped being limited by the constraints placed on him by nature. He began \u201cmastering\u201d nature at that point. I could see that as being something that caused Griffin to decide man had trespassed against a law of Gaia. Since agriculture is tied to the Impergium, it also makes a lot of sense.<br /> <br /> Being the divine punisher of men for a specific crime (whether mastery of fire, or beginning agriculture) also helps water down the general hatred of mankind for the Red Talons. Their original \u201crole\u201d was not to exterminate man. They wanted to enforce divine retribution for Man's transgression. This keeps the anger and hostility towards Mankind in general, but channels it to particular individuals or groups instead of the species as a whole. That punishment could be poetic reprisals against individuals; or enforce a few collective punishments. Not wholesale slaughter of every single human (which should be limited to the Lodge of the Predator Kings). One does not waste, squander, or misdirect divine judgment or vengeance. That would be impious. Instead, it is brought on the person who violated that taboo. For bringing fire to mankind, Zeus did not punish men, he punished Prometheus specifically. (OK, Zeus also tricked Pandora to unleash evil against mankind.) However, such a myth would give the Red Talons a basis for opposing the help other tribes (particularly the Children of Gaia, Glass Walkers, and Bone Gnawers) gives men to overcome those evils. They are going against Gaia's ordained punishment of Mankind.<br /> <br /> At the same time, the fear of not wanting to misdirect divine judgment of specific individuals (against collective mankind) could serve as a good reason to explain why Red Talon \u201cmoderates\u201d are holding back the Lodge of the Predator Kings from open warfare with humanity. There might be all sorts of taboos and spiritual explanations against open warfare even though the bulk of Red Talons detest humanity. Instead of a general hate, these Red Talons could point that Griffin's ire is specifically directed towards certain behaviour, and that Red Talons can have some relationship with humans who don't violate certain taboos, or who may offer proper chiminage in recompense to minor offenses.<br /> <br /> Of course, nowadays the existence of pure hunter-gatherers is very limited so there is practically no one who would not be considered to be a target. But I still like the taboo-violator idea as it provides some sort of basis for Red Talons to not seek outright extermination of mankind, or at least give some window for certain Red Talons to utilize the homid form and interact with man.<br /> <br /> It also provides a basis for the Warders of the Land as something other than just the boring example of &quot;ordinary&quot; Red Talons. Perhaps there is something more specific about the type of land they ward. Something that keeps them away from the bulk of humanity so that it limits the interaction between the tribe and humanity, despite the Red Talons desire to want to go to war.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-11T21:16:17-07:00","id":912631,"title":"","content":"<b>Griffins love Gold</b><br /> <br /> Griffins are known for guarding treasure and priceless possessions. Griffins love gold.<br /> <br /> The supposed love of gold by griffins, and other attributes of protecting treasure, is similar to dragons. In both cases, it represents protecting the natural wealth of the world in its purest, un-mined and unrefined forms. This ties back to the idea that the wilderness and nature are not to be spoiled, but left alone.<br /> <br /> The love of gold by Mankind is certainly a strong theme, and Griffin may seek to protect it precisely in order to deny it to Mankind, perhaps because it knows the love of gold allows evil to enter the hearts of men. Their love of gold also represents a possible source to use for chiminage and rites \u2013 returning gold to griffin (in essence destroying the wealth or surplus bounty of Mankind; ritually helping return man to his original, primitive state) pleases him.<br /> <br /> This could have other symbolic consequences. If Griffin wants to destroy the wealth of mankind, historically the best way for wolves to do that is attack livestock. Perhaps a way for very young Red Talon cubs (and perhaps older Garou too) to earn favor/pay chiminage to Griffin's brood for their Gifts is to seize a nearby farmer's chickens, sheep, etc.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T04:02:50-07:00","id":912725,"title":"","content":"As much as I like your ideas, I do think they are taking the tribe a bit too far from their concept. Red Talons are meant to be the opposite of Glass Walkers, the wolves to their humans. The problem with them is, that it ended up putting them in a very narrow role. Not helped by the fact that some groups barely can deal with lupus, let alone Talons.<br /> <br /> There is also the fact that there is a hilariously big focus on the fact that Red Talons kill humans. Ignoring entirely that -all- garou kill humans, even to protect their territory. Again, not helped by the bizarre christmas tree from 2nd ed tribebook.<br /> <br /> I think for Talons, it would be good to focus on what they can give to the Nation as a whole. What secrets of the wilds only they can use.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T06:23:59-07:00","id":912763,"title":"","content":"Once again Black Fox, you did it again. I really like these <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> you did it with Unicorn really interestingly and now you Griffin. I really hope this becomes a series where you expand on others Totems too.<br /> <br /> As the first sentence says in the starting post &quot;discuss ideas on how I'd develop it a little differently&quot; and you certainly do that.<br /> <br /> I have thoughts on each of your ideas here (which I all like, for different reasons) which I'll share once I have a little more time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7706-somuchlove","name":"Somuchlove","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7706_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T07:44:23-07:00","id":912793,"title":"","content":"Predation is a non-specific activity that includes herbivory and filter-feeding. Carnivory is more what you're looking for on that corner.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912631#post912631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Griffins love Gold</b><br /> <br /> Griffins are known for guarding treasure and priceless possessions. Griffins love gold.<br /> <br /> The supposed love of gold by griffins, and other attributes of protecting treasure, is similar to dragons. In both cases, it represents protecting the natural wealth of the world in its purest, un-mined and unrefined forms. This ties back to the idea that the wilderness and nature are not to be spoiled, but left alone.<br /> <br /> The love of gold by Mankind is certainly a strong theme, and Griffin may seek to protect it precisely in order to deny it to Mankind, perhaps because it knows the love of gold allows evil to enter the hearts of men. Their love of gold also represents a possible source to use for chiminage and rites \u2013 returning gold to griffin (in essence destroying the wealth or surplus bounty of Mankind; ritually helping return man to his original, primitive state) pleases him.<br /> <br /> This could have other symbolic consequences. If Griffin wants to destroy the wealth of mankind, historically the best way for wolves to do that is attack livestock. Perhaps a way for very young Red Talon cubs (and perhaps older Garou too) to earn favor/pay chiminage to Griffin's brood for their Gifts is to seize a nearby farmer's chickens, sheep, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is related to another angle that Werewolf plays from time to time, incidentally.<br /> <br /> <b>Griffins are thought to be based on stories of partially broken <i>Protoceratops </i>fossils, which often showed up near gold deposits.</b><br /> <br /> Break the frill off the skull and scatter it around the shoulders, and you have what looks like a wing on a four-footed, beaked animal. This could be something that an otherwise Lost One totem might have used to break out of the darkness and take on patronizing a tribe, the better to prevent the mistakes of the past that led to the Dragon Wars from happening anew. Unfortunately, of course, the clock has run out on that, and thus the totem rages even harder. And, of course, there could be another Mokole connection there, between the gold and the revitalized, would-have-otherwise-been-a-ghost totem.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T12:11:25-07:00","id":912918,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ana Mizuki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912725#post912725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As much as I like your ideas, I do think they are taking the tribe a bit too far from their concept. Red Talons are meant to be the opposite of Glass Walkers, the wolves to their humans.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is interesting because in my eyes, nothing of what I've posted has really taken the Red Talons away from their &quot;core&quot; concept. Even though these are &quot;heretical&quot; thoughts, meaning they are not what is depicted as canon, I do my best to keep the general attitude or concept of the tribe the same - at least as they were originally written in the corebook even if not the tribebooks.<br /> <br /> The most obvious way to begin this is to look at their actual totem and see what real myths and legends are associated with them, and then figure out how to include them.<br /> <br /> The Red Talons is a problematic tribe because most people don't know how to include them in a game. Part of my reason for this thread, is to try to find ways to open up the Talons and give them some additional depth and variety. At the same time, I want them to be recognizably Red Talons.<br /> <br /> The Red Talons kill humans aspect is extremely one dimensional. However, it makes no sense that they are trying to kill all humans right now. Even though the World of Darkness is a variant of our world with lots of more terrible things, ongoing massacres of humans just can't happen without it making the world so different it becomes unrecognizable. So while I want to keep the antagonism and hate towards humans, the actual killing needs to become something that is manageable.<br /> <br /> Otherwise the result becomes that the Talons are all talk and no walk. For all their speech about wiping out mankind and going to war, what are they actually doing? Are any settlements being wiped out in the depths of the Canadian or Siberian wilderness? If they were, wouldn't this become huge news? How many people have ever played games where this actually happened - even as background noise much less as part of the main story for the PCs? Instead of being a feared bunch of Garou, they become pathetic losers who don't actually follow through.<br /> <br /> Neither scenario is good for the game.<br /> <br /> The game has not been consistent in its depiction of the tribe. They were originally presented as all genocidal except for a few members. Then when camps were introduced, it seemed to put the real exterminationists in the Lodge of the Predator Kings, put the bulk of the tribe in the Warders as &quot;moderates&quot; who are hostile, but not actively killing, and then a small faction of mostly younger Garou who are curious about humans in the Whelps Compromise. Then this distinction is completely forgotten and the entire tribe portrayed as all killing humans.<br /> <br /> I think the tribe, as depicted as canon, doesn't really work. I think many people like the general concept though, but struggle to figure out how to include them (even as NPCs in their chronicles, much less as PCs!).<br /> <br /> I'm trying to come up with things that explain both the Red Talons hatred of humanity, and the fact they really don't go on genocidal rampages as suggested in the book. Somehow these things need to be reconciled - the extreme position written in the books, and the almost complete absence of attacks in everyone's chronicles.<br /> <br /> I'll be posting new ideas later on, and hopefully they'll be something you can use.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T12:29:26-07:00","id":912926,"title":"","content":"From what I'm reading, maybe the issue is keeping Griffin at all? Would changing the tribe's totem be more effective? Like from Griffin to the spirit-with-no-name?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T12:34:03-07:00","id":912931,"title":"","content":"I'm pretty sure my dislike of the Red Talons as written is well documented on the forums by now.<br /> <br /> This isn't the direction I've advocated pushing the Tribe towards (though it isn't incompatible either), but as far as I'm concerned it is miles above the books in concept. While the refocusing of anger towards a divine retribution against specific individuals is a cool way to keep the &quot;wolves angry at men&quot; aspect that's essential to the basic Tribe in the books, I'm actually much more intrigued in sussing out the idea of Red Talons as - in some ways - the flip side of the Shadow Lords as well as the Glasswalkers. The Shadow Lords' Machiavellian and social Darwinist mentalities - esp. regarding their relationship to the Silver Fangs - is very human. The Red Talons serving as a &quot;wolf&quot; contrast to this seems really compelling to me.<br /> <br /> As well, the idea of there being significant numbers of Red Talons that seek to - in a positive fashion - &quot;proselytize&quot; to the lupus of other Tribes is an interesting one to add to their appeal in a wider range of applications within the game. There's a lot of potential internal Red Talon debating over what to do (ex: is their goal to remind non-Talon lupus how to be &quot;real&quot; lupus, or to try to use non-Talon lupus as in roads to getting their non-lupus Tribemates to act in better accordance with the Red Talon philosophy), as well as a lot more that can be done with the Talons as inter-Tribal actors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T14:17:04-07:00","id":912993,"title":"","content":"Just want to preface this by saying that I fully agree with you that the tribe needs cleaning up ASAP. Just noting the why's here.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912918#post912918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That is interesting because in my eyes, nothing of what I've posted has really taken the Red Talons away from their &quot;core&quot; concept. Even though these are &quot;heretical&quot; thoughts, meaning they are not what is depicted as canon, I do my best to keep the general attitude or concept of the tribe the same - at least as they were originally written in the corebook even if not the tribebooks.<br /> <br /> The most obvious way to begin this is to look at their actual totem and see what real myths and legends are associated with them, and then figure out how to include them.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, that's the thing, the Talons are supposed to be -so- removed from humans that they don't even match the myths or consider them at all. It is all 'human mind' stuff to them.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912918#post912918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Red Talons kill humans aspect is extremely one dimensional. However, it makes no sense that they are trying to kill all humans right now. Even though the World of Darkness is a variant of our world with lots of more terrible things, ongoing massacres of humans just can't happen without it making the world so different it becomes unrecognizable. So while I want to keep the antagonism and hate towards humans, the actual killing needs to become something that is manageable.</div> </div> </div> </div>To this, I think the numbers were supposed to be low due to how few Talons there are. As for why they kill them, they are essentially trying to take as many humans with them as they slowly die out.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912918#post912918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Otherwise the result becomes that the Talons are all talk and no walk. For all their speech about wiping out mankind and going to war, what are they actually doing? Are any settlements being wiped out in the depths of the Canadian or Siberian wilderness? If they were, wouldn't this become huge news? How many people have ever played games where this actually happened - even as background noise much less as part of the main story for the PCs? Instead of being a feared bunch of Garou, they become pathetic losers who don't actually follow through.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, there are so few they cannot make much of an impact. Sure, they can attack few cabins. But that is about it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912918#post912918\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the tribe, as depicted as canon, doesn't really work. I think many people like the general concept though, but struggle to figure out how to include them (even as NPCs in their chronicles, much less as PCs!).</div> </div> </div> </div>There is also the fact that they are all lupus, and to some, it becomes a pretty big red flag just for that. While I've been games that have been fine with lupus, there have also been some that outright treat lupus as twinks by default.<br /> <br /> Now imagine a tribe solely of lupus hating humans, it is very easy for players to see them as the bad guys. Especially in online games, the focus on homid-side is so strong that just having a Red Talon is hard to deal with. Few have managed, sure, but most see them as always -trouble- characters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T14:28:43-07:00","id":912999,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912931#post912931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm actually much more intrigued in sussing out the idea of Red Talons as - in some ways - the flip side of the Shadow Lords as well as the Glasswalkers. The Shadow Lords' Machiavellian and social Darwinist mentalities - esp. regarding their relationship to the Silver Fangs - is very human. The Red Talons serving as a &quot;wolf&quot; contrast to this seems really compelling to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm glad you liked that. I don't think I developed it as well as I should have in the original post, but your description is exactly what I was thinking of. That is a good hook for modern games - what the Talons are doing now.<br /> <br /> One aspect of this that just occurred to me is that while the idea of the Red Talons as a &quot;leadership tribe&quot; may seem like nonsense now, it might not have been for most of Garou history. Maybe in 8000 BC or 4000 BC or even 400 BC, the Garou saw the Red Talons as one of the leaders of the Nation just like some of the other tribes now. Maybe a modern day Garou historian going through oral legends and history of important Concolations notice that Red Talon leaders were given a lot more prominence and role in them than they are now. The Red Talons might even have been considered to be the &quot;beta tribe&quot; second only to the Silver Fangs for a long time, or at least one of other leader tribes that supported the Silver Fangs. As their numbers dwindled though, so did their influence. <br /> <br /> Maybe there has been an intentional or unintentional erasing of the prominence of the Red Talons in Garou history,and their importance in creating the Litany, concolations, and other important events. It might be intentional in that certain tribes want to make their own contributions seem more important, or to downplay prestige and influence the tribe had historically in order to discount their views now. I can see the Children of Gaia doing that. Unintentionally is that with the decline of lupus stock, the lupus perspective is discounted and the homids play up the Garou whose mentalities make more sense to homids.<br /> <br /> This has several consequences for modern games. One is that the Talons would remember this even if other tribes do not. They have kept alive a lot of the old stories forgotten by others. Characters who are researching history may want to go to Red Talon septs to learn these old stories. This could have importance for more than just a flavor text of a bitter people remembering &quot;when we were kings&quot;. The Red Talons might remember stories of lore of certain monsters, great banes, and other enemies that were active in 6500 BC and how to defeat them, that the other tribes forgot. When the PCs learn of the great evil the ST intends for them to stop, they're only hope of learning the macguffin that can defeat it is to find out what Red Talon remembers this, especially since the sept the story is most associated with was destroyed more than two thousand years ago when the city of Lyon was founded by the Gauls.<br /> <br /> Another is the role of Ancestors for Red Talon characters. Since so many other Backgrounds don't make sense for Red Talon characters, Ancestors is probably ranked highly. In terms of Ancestors and Pure Breed, the Red Talons probably are among the strongest of all the Garou tribes. Putting an emphasis on the Ancestor spirits that Red Talons are in communion with is another way to distinguish the tribe. Perhaps Red Talons routinely let Ancestors &quot;ride&quot; them, and this is an important part of Red Talon culture and sept activity. Perhaps some of the stories involving human sacrifice and the like is a requisite of chiminage for such Ancestor spirits?<br /> <br /> Last would be that this could be the basis for a story in a chronicle. Maybe there is a member of another tribe - a Shadow Lord or Children of Gaia - going to sept to sept to educate them on Garou history with an edited form of a legend which omits the Red Talon contributions in preparation for a future concolation, or some other political plot. Or a Red Talon galliard who travels sept to sept with the &quot;correct&quot; version that homids have forgotten. Maybe one or the other is secretly killed to prevent the &quot;wrong&quot; version coming out. It could be the basis for a nice murder mystery or big political plot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-13T21:24:53-07:00","id":913856,"title":"","content":"Here is another one.<br /> <br /> <b>Griffin's Brood of Wisdom</b><br /> <br /> Similar creatures exist like the Anzu, Sphinx (particularly hieracosphinxes), Simurgh, Ziz, and Lamassu/Shedu. Indeed, Sphinx and Simurgh are listed as part of Griffin's brood in Axis Mundi. So we can also look to these legendary creatures for inspiration. These creatures are mainly associated with wisdom.<br /> <br /> But what kind of wisdom? This can't be the kind of rational or philosophical thinking preferred by homids or the Stargazers. This has to be wisdom gained through instinct or what the Taoists call \u201cno-mind\u201d or others cal crazy wisdom, not contemplation or rational thinking. This would be a wisdom totem of Primal Urge and one's animal instincts. The inborn and intuitive responses. Wisdom not from the Weaver's thinking mind, but of the Wyld's unconscious mind which means dreams, insights, creative inspiration, and gut instincts. Lateral thinking (solving problems by an indirect and creative approach, typically through viewing the problem in a new and unusual light) instead of step-by-step logic. Huge leaps of logic that nonetheless leads to the correct conclusion. These might be Red Talon theurges uttering koans and acting like Buddhist gurus, or Red Talon ragabashes acting like contrary warriors and speaking in riddles.<br /> <br /> There would be a separate Red Talon \u201cwisdom tradition\u201d within the Garou Nation. It would probably appeal a lot to Lupus Garou, but I could also see homids and other tribes seeking out renown Red Talons wisdom teachers, especially from Stargazers and Uktena. I could even see Gallairds coming to such teachers hoping to rediscover lost creativity or overcome an artistic block.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 07-03-2016, 02:40 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-13T21:40:27-07:00","id":913860,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=913856#post913856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here is another one.<br /> <br /> <b>Griffin's Brood of Wisdom</b><br /> <br /> Similar creatures exist like the Anzu, Sphinx (particularly hieracosphinxes), Simurgh, Ziz, and Lamassu/Shedu. Indeed, Sphinx and Simurgh are listed as part of Griffin's brood in Axis Mundi. So we can also look to these legendary creatures for inspiration. These creatures are mainly associated with wisdom.<br /> <br /> But what kind of wisdom? This can't be the kind of rational or philosophical thinking preferred by homids or the Stargazers. This has to be wisdom gained through instinct or what the Taoists call \u201cno-mind\u201d or others cal crzy wisdom, not contemplation or rational thinking. This would be a wisdom totem of Primal Urge and one's animal instincts. The inborn and intuitive responses. Wisdom not from the Weaver's thinking mind, but of the Wyld's unconscious mind which means dreams, insights, creative inspiration, and gut instincts. Lateral thinking (solving problems by an indirect and creative approach, typically through viewing the problem in a new and unusual light) instead of step-by-step logic. Huge leaps of logic that nonetheless leads to the correct conclusion. These might be Red Talon theurges uttering koans and acting like Buddhist gurus, or Red Talon ragabashes acting like contrary warriors and speaking in riddles.<br /> <br /> There would be a separate Red Talon \u201cwisdom tradition\u201d within the Garou Nation. It would probably appeal a lot to Lupus Garou, but I could also see homids and other tribes seeking out renown Red Talons wisdom teachers, especially from Stargazers and Uktena. I could even see Gallairds coming to such teachers hoping to rediscover lost creativity or overcome an artistic block.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't say that it comes from the Wyld; though the Red Talons are the ideological opposites of the Glass Walkers in most ways, this doesn't need to mean embracing the Wyld. Shield of Gaia conceptualizes this as being in-tune with the true laws of Gaia, rather than coming from any part of the Triat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-15T20:50:33-07:00","id":915347,"title":"","content":"<b>Protector Against Evil and Slander</b><br /> <br /> The Achaemenid Persians considered the griffin &quot;a protector from evil, witchcraft and secret slander&quot;.<br /> <br /> Griffin as a general &quot;protector from evil, witchcraft and secret slander&quot; is an interesting angle, especially since humans, not animals, would want to evoke that. That aspect, although unusual, need not be a dealbreaker for Griffin's traditional hatred or dislike for humans. There is always \u201cdeep magic\u201d going on.<br /> <br /> If that is an aspect of Griffin, then the appropriate chiminage and so on makes it happen. Perhaps this role happened very early on before Griffin determined it needed to punish man. If we consider agriculture as the taboo crime, then it theoretically becomes possible Griffin might have allowed early hunting pre-agriculture man to offer chiminage in exchange for limited protection.<br /> <br /> If Griffin is a protective spirit, then we have some intriguing ideas for the Warders of the Land camp as Warders are responsible for protecting what area they \u201cward\u201d. This gives this camp a bit more to do than simply being bland ordinary default Red Talons. The camp might possess certain secrets and defenses that counter \u201cevil\u201d or \u201cwitchcraft\u201d particularly that which affects nature (including the agriculture of men). Droughts, disease, fierce storms and floodings, earthquakes, and other natural disasters are in the purview of this camp. Such natural disasters were associated with witches and malevolent magic.<br /> <br /> Protecting against \u201csecret slander\u201d is also an interesting choice for the Red Talons, but also appropriate for a tribe not known for their guile and following a particularly regal and majestic totem. I think it also gives them an additional role to play within the Garou Nation \u2013 a protector of one's reputation, and therefore Honor. If the Red Talons, or at least the dominant camp among them, is somehow bound against \u201csecret slander\u201d it makes them a particularly trustworthy group. Whatever their faults, they do not lie or cheat behind your back. There is some kind of role here for a Red Talon philodox. I imagine this camp makes them not particularly liked by many Shadow Lords whose preferred method of attack against their political foes is often \u201csecret slander\u201d.<br /> <br /> There is a tie here to the earlier post as a guardian of the divine, and this aspect is probably a sub-aspect of that. It is the idea of a &quot;natural order&quot; that Griffin upholds, and when it is violated in other forms, Griffin still goes after such a trespasser or violator.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-17T21:54:39-07:00","id":916647,"title":"","content":"<b>Steeds of the Gods</b><br /> <br /> Griffins were the steeds of the gods, having pulled the chariots of Apollo, Zeus and Nemesis. In Dante's 'Divine Comedy', the Sacred Gryphon pulls the chariot of the Church to take the narrator to heaven.<br /> <br /> If griffins are the steeds of the gods, then griffins represent the conveyance of the divine powers to the earthly realm. Indeed, the Red Talons have been repeatedly mentioned as a source of divine prophecy for the Garou Nation. Griffin brings what is in the Heavens down to the Realm, and this is an important function of the spirit. It explains why Red Talon prophets bring word to the Garou glimpses of the future or warnings despite this tribe not being particularly known for their Theurges.<br /> <br /> As a corollary, the Griffin Incarna and its jagglings would be known for their travel between the various Umbras. They routinely go into the High Umbra and interact with beings there. Maybe that is the same for the Dreaming. Although the Red Talons are not known for their ties to the Dreaming, it is easy to imagine members of that tribe being responsible for the Big Bad Wolf characters in so many fairy tales.<br /> <br /> Lupus have higher Gnosis than homids, so lupus characters should be played with that in mind. As a tribe of all lupus, Red Talons collectively have a much stronger tie to Gaia than any other tribe since most of that tribe's members do not have the large amount of Gnosis that lupus have. Red Talons have a much stronger tie to the spirit world than the other tribes, even compared to the Uktena and Stargazers who are probably seen by most players as the \u201cspiritual\u201d tribes. Yet this connection is overlooked.<br /> <br /> Building on this motif, Griffin packs and Red Talon theurges could have a heritage of really skilled umbral travelers, particularly to appropriate Near Realms or even other local penumbras in the Dreaming or Astral Plane. Perhaps they do this in the hope of catching a glimpse of Griffin in his voyages among the gods so they can receive a vision.<br /> <br /> Red Talons should be spending a lot of their time in the umbra. The game makes a point that the spiritual world is more important than the physical. Spirits can survive the death or destruction of their counterparts in the Realm, but spiritual destruction inevitably leads to physical death. Given this, we can see the Red Talons look to solutions to the problems facing them not in the Realm, but in the Umbra. Whatever bad things happen in the Realm have a \u201ctrue source\u201d in the Umbra. Any cause and effect relationship that takes place in the Realm is really of minor importance. The real solutions lie in the spirit world. If that can be fixed, then the Realm will straighten itself out.<br /> <br /> I think that is a good perspective for some of the Red Talons ideas of war against humanity. Many of the battlefronts of that war don't happen in the Realm, and it isn't fought against individual human individuals. Instead it is often fought on battlegrounds in the Umbra. Maybe it is against Weaver spirits responsible for the wealth and power of human civilization; or against the spiritual forces of the Wyrm that disconnect man from his ties to Gaia; or against malevolent Banes engaged in individual plots. The Red Talons might be seen as spiritual warriors par excellence. The kind of allies other septs want when they need to raid the enemy strongholds in the Umbra.<br /> <br /> An emphasis on Red Talon activity in the Umbra also allows the ST to showcase the Red Talons in an environment where their war with humanity will not disrupt the game. Instead of being an example of what is &quot;bad&quot; with the Garou, it would be an example of what is &quot;good&quot; with the Garou. The ST can emphasize a very deep spiritual, even benevolent side of the Red Talons. Perhaps many spirits show a favorable bias to Red Talons and packs of Griffin as a result of the strong spiritual ties and honest chiminage provided by them to the spirits. It might even shame PCs that they aren't more like the Talons!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 06-18-2016, 01:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T07:29:14-07:00","id":916822,"title":"","content":"Perhaps the Talons have cultivated a kind of Gnostic world view where humans are collectively the demiurge that has stolen away the true Gaia from them? Accordingly, this imprisons the Talons - and wolves as a whole - in a world of stifling materiality, where their kin are slaughtered, powerless before what should not be wielded by the humans, but is.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-18T10:13:36-07:00","id":916885,"title":"","content":"Interesting. It is true that the Realm and Umbra were one before the Gauntlet, and it was activities by humans that created it. I don't think they'd see humans as a Demiurge though - that would be the role of the Weaver. Humans could be seen as the corrupt servants of the Weaver though. Or perhaps there is some mythical Incarna really responsible for that - like how the Black Furies blame everything on the &quot;Patriarch&quot;. Maybe the Red Talons call it the Machine or the Gauntlet Raiser. I wonder what would be a good name for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T22:18:03-07:00","id":917801,"title":"","content":"<b>Griffins hate horses, and horses are their favorite prey</b><br /> <br /> The idea that griffins hate horses can be shown in the original conception of the hippogriff, a cross between griffins and horses. While today a hippogriff is just another fantasy creature, originally the term was used to mean a concept that could not exist. The ancients thought a cross between griffin and horse to be impossible because it was well known that griffins hated horses and would attack and kill them.<br /> <br /> Griffin\u2019s hatred of horses might be a result of horses being one of the important domesticated animals of man. Perhaps horses are considered traitors to the animal world. If so, griffin likely hates all the other domesticated animals as well \u2013 dogs, cattle, cats, chickens, pigs, etc.<br /> <br /> Horses were probably domesticated by man around 3500 BC on the Eurasian Steppes. This time would be after the end of the Impergium as the great cities of Mesopotamia built on intensive agriculture were in existence by then. Perhaps Griffin was much angrier at the horse for being domesticated at this time, when man had violated the laws of Gaia (via agriculture), than he was with the earlier domestication of other species (when man was merely a hunter-gatherer). I also think it has something to do with what domestication of the horse did to humanity. Earlier domesticated simply provided man with food to eat. Horses for the first time allowed man to travel faster and gain an advantage over animals for hunting. Man therefore became much more dangerous, especially to wolves and other predators. We can imagine legends of the Red Talons when the Griffin incarna approached the Horse incarna, and they fought over Horse's betrayal of the rest of the animal kingdom.<br /> <br /> This might make good ways to teach Red Talon cubs aspects of the tribe. Send them out in raiding parties to attack hen houses, cattle, sheep, etc. which is all natural wolf behaviour anyway. But what was once only a means to fill your belly, now becomes symbolic spiritual warfare against the spirits who \u201cbetrayed\u201d Gaia by agreeing to become mankind's servants and slaves. Spirits of these animals may even be the subject of attack by Red Talons in the Umbra.<br /> <br /> Perhaps some of the stories homids tell of the Red Talons ritual killing of men are actually that of abducted horses sacrificed to appease Griffin. It might be a good way to show PCs the extent of the Red Talon anger and rage without provoking PCs to intervene and stop Talons. It would also preserve some of the bloodier and more horrific Red Talon rituals, but not be as offensive as PCs or create as much difficulty as introducing NPCs into a chronicle.<br /> <br /> We can also imagine Red Talons encouraging domesticated animals to escape man and live in the wild, becoming feral. I imagine Griffin would not have as much trouble with mustangs - feral horses - as he would with domesticated kind. They might become friends with feral cats and dogs, mustangs, pigeons, razorbacks, and other feral animals. Indeed, the Red Talons might lead missions inside towns and cities in order to encourage animals to abandon their servitude among men and leave free once again, or protect such feral animals from dangers. Perhaps some Red Talon Galliards sneak into cities and use Beast Speech to learn news and rumors from such \u201cspies\u201d. Or they come back and tell their septs the stories of such abandoned animals as more proof of man's ways. In Axis Mundi, \u201cLost Dogs\u201d belong to the brood of Rat so we can imagine many spirits of feral spirits are likewise loyal to Rat. This may make some interesting conflict between Red Talons and Bone Gnawers depending on the exact type of feral spirits involved.<br /> <br /> Maybe Red Talon theurges lead debating packs into the Umbra to find the spirits of domesticated animals and argue to convince them to repudiate their loyalty to man and once again align with the Wyld. Plots that could arise from this might be similar to the story in The Birds (both the Du Maurier book and Hitchcock film) when avians began mysteriously attacking humans. Small scale local phenomonen similar to that might happen whenever Red Talons win such a debate with a local jaggling. There are lots of possibilities for stories that begin with \u201cthe animals are acting weird\u201d. A Red Talon plot where they succeed with an Incarna (or come close to it) might be the basis of an entire chronicle.<br /> <br /> I like this because it gives additional ways for Red Talons to fight Mankind without the need of violence that threatens the Veil (which I find makes the ST's job ever harder and often damages suspension of disbelief). Instead, we have plots in the Umbra, and strange animal activity in the Realm.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-20T11:45:35-07:00","id":918159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912630#post912630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, nowadays the existence of pure hunter-gatherers is very limited so there is practically no one who would not be considered to be a target. But I still like the taboo-violator idea as it provides some sort of basis for Red Talons to not seek outright extermination of mankind, or at least give some window for certain Red Talons to utilize the homid form and interact with man.<br /> <br /> It also provides a basis for the Warders of the Land as something other than just the boring example of &quot;ordinary&quot; Red Talons. Perhaps there is something more specific about the type of land they ward. Something that keeps them away from the bulk of humanity so that it limits the interaction between the tribe and humanity, despite the Red Talons desire to want to go to war.</div> </div> </div> </div>On that whole 'modern day hunters are a thing of the past, so the whole 'agriculture' is bupkis angle. Perhaps modern developers, campers who create forest fires, strip miners and the like are their new focus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912630#post912630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The love of gold by Mankind is certainly a strong theme, and Griffin may seek to protect it precisely in order to deny it to Mankind, perhaps because it knows the love of gold allows evil to enter the hearts of men. Their love of gold also represents a possible source to use for chiminage and rites \u2013 returning gold to griffin (in essence destroying the wealth or surplus bounty of Mankind; ritually helping return man to his original, primitive state) pleases him.<br /> <br /> This could have other symbolic consequences. If Griffin wants to destroy the wealth of mankind, historically the best way for wolves to do that is attack livestock. Perhaps a way for very young Red Talon cubs (and perhaps older Garou too) to earn favor/pay chiminage to Griffin's brood for their Gifts is to seize a nearby farmer's chickens, sheep, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>I love that angle, specially on the Chiminage..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912630#post912630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Otherwise the result becomes that the Talons are all talk and no walk. For all their speech about wiping out mankind and going to war, what are they actually doing? Are any settlements being wiped out in the depths of the Canadian or Siberian wilderness? If they were, wouldn't this become huge news? How many people have ever played games where this actually happened - even as background noise much less as part of the main story for the PCs? Instead of being a feared bunch of Garou, they become pathetic losers who don't actually follow through.</div> </div> </div> </div>I've actually had one game where that DID happen.. During a major winter storm though, so the RT's could hope the news foisted it off on being weather related..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CeltSPZ</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912926#post912926\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From what I'm reading, maybe the issue is keeping Griffin at all? Would changing the tribe's totem be more effective? Like from Griffin to the spirit-with-no-name?</div> </div> </div> </div>Good point. Perhaps a totem Change is in order..<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=912630#post912630\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Griffin\u2019s hatred of horses might be a result of horses being one of the important domesticated animals of man. Perhaps horses are considered traitors to the animal world. If so, griffin likely hates all the other domesticated animals as well \u2013 dogs, cattle, cats, chickens, pigs, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, aren't cats hated anyway by wolves??","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-20T18:18:55-07:00","id":918354,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=918159#post918159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, aren't cats hated anyway by wolves??</div> </div> </div> </div>No? Or are we talking about Garou and Bastet?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-20T19:18:42-07:00","id":918369,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>garhkal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=918159#post918159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've actually had one game where that DID happen.. During a major winter storm though, so the RT's could hope the news foisted it off on being weather related..</div> </div> </div> </div>Very cool. I am a very big believer that players (NPCs too, but granted that is much easier) really need to honor the Veil (and Masquerade and all the other laws that hide supernaturals). That means creating believable cover stories. The use of natural disasters is a good way to do this for the Garou.<br /> <br /> Glad you are enjoying some of the ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-21T00:11:51-07:00","id":918470,"title":"","content":"What they didn't count on was how they were gonna explain the slashed up dead bodies they left in many houses though.. So they piled them all up in one room in each house, and burned them up... so eventually it looked like a 'heater caught the house on fire and they died/got burned up....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11674-garhkal","name":"garhkal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11674_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"547"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-22T22:24:42-07:00","id":919514,"title":"","content":"<b>Griffons Mate for Life</b><br /> <br /> In legend, griffins not only mated for life, but if either partner died, then the other would continue the rest of its life alone, never to search for a new mate. The griffin was thus made an emblem of the Christian Church's opposition to remarriage.<br /> <br /> The loyalty of the Griffin to their mate even after death might have echoes in Griffin\u2019s adoption of the spirits of animals that have gone extinct. While the actual living creatures themselves have left Gaia, Griffon may consider it his duty to protect their spirits because of his role as a protector of the sacred spaces of Gaia. Even though they have passed on and their children have left the Realm, Griffin continues to be loyal to them.<br /> <br /> Loyalty may also explain the Red Talons continued following of the Silver Fangs despite the obvious decline of the Garou people during the last thousand years. Often this is displayed as a result of the sense of hierarchy among wolves and submission to the alpha, but perhaps this has a deeper reason as a Child of Griffin. After all, in a wolf pack the leaders will be challenged once they get old and weak. A natural wolf instinct would be to depose the Silver Fangs if they seem like they are not hacking it. If Griffin expects loyalty from his children though, this might complicate things for the Red Talons.<br /> <br /> In terms of mating, this may also impact the Red Talons. Wolves tend to be mate in loyal pairs (often just the alpha pair, but in some packs another pair may also mate), but if one wolf dies the other often finds another mate. Also, not all mates are loyal. Some males may bond with different females in different years.<br /> <br /> However, among the Red Talons they may follow Griffin much more closely. Red Talons may mate with only one partner wolf. And since Garou live far longer than wolves, this would have a huge impact on the tribe's population. In fact, this would have such a huge impact, it is probably not the case. However, it may mean that 1) while their mate is alive, a Red Talon would never mate with another, and 2) some Red Talons may take it upon themselves to only truly mate with only one wolf their entire lives as an offering or sacrifice to Griffin. It would be the equivalent of a Red Talon monk. <br /> <br /> A critical question is how do Red Talons interpret their own mating behavior. In wolf packs, typically only the alpha pair mate and the others do not. Is this wolf characteristic carry over to Red Talon lupus as well, or does this not carry over to Garou? I have to imagine that Red Talons do not reproduce wolf behavior here. This may cause them some anguish and feeling isolated from their wolf kinfolk - which is actually quite appropriate for Garou. Hearing a Red Talon lament their isolation from their own wolf kin could humanize a tribe generally known for their hatred of Man.<br /> <br /> I think this is some interesting flavor for the tribe. While somewhat based on real wolf behavior, this actually seems more like a human trait. Furthermore, this contrasts sharply with a lot of other mating behavior of the other tribes; some of whom seem to believe to use kinfolk as breeding stock as much as they can. This would portray the Red Talons in a much more positive, even romantic, light.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-23T09:14:18-07:00","id":919757,"title":"","content":"From what I understand, the alpha wolf phenomenon is a product of artificially formed wolf packs. In the wild, wolf packs are actually family units and don't have a hierarchy so much as they have a mother, a father, and a bunch of cubs of various ages. There really isn't much in the way of challenging the alpha for control of the pack, it would be pretty bad for genetic diversity.<br /> <br /> Given that, you may have just brought forward one of the biggest points of conflict between the Red Talons and their Patron. In general, Werewolves can't fill traditional obligations for either human or wolf society. Mating for life implies a lot more than just a quick action then years of abandonment. Perhaps Griffin's charge is something the Red Talons can't maintain, so on some level he is always angry with them, which in turn stokes the fury of the tribe as a whole. On the flip side, maybe the Red Talons actually do find mates and bond with them and bring them into their own packs. In a strange turnabout it could be the Red Talons who keep the biggest ties to the mortal world by bringing their families into the conflict.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-23T10:10:19-07:00","id":919785,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=919757#post919757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From what I understand, the alpha wolf phenomenon is a product of artificially formed wolf packs. In the wild, wolf packs are actually family units and don't have a hierarchy so much as they have a mother, a father, and a bunch of cubs of various ages. There really isn't much in the way of challenging the alpha for control of the pack, it would be pretty bad for genetic diversity.<br /> <br /> Given that, you may have just brought forward one of the biggest points of conflict between the Red Talons and their Patron. In general, Werewolves can't fill traditional obligations for either human or wolf society. Mating for life implies a lot more than just a quick action then years of abandonment. Perhaps Griffin's charge is something the Red Talons can't maintain, so on some level he is always angry with them, which in turn stokes the fury of the tribe as a whole. On the flip side, maybe the Red Talons actually do find mates and bond with them and bring them into their own packs. In a strange turnabout it could be the Red Talons who keep the biggest ties to the mortal world by bringing their families into the conflict.</div> </div> </div> </div>Red Talon families are all wolves. They're part of the conflict whether the Red Talons bring them into it or not, on account of teetering on the brink and frequently being the targets of both directed enemy action and human issues..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-23T10:51:53-07:00","id":919811,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=919785#post919785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Red Talon families are all wolves. They're part of the conflict whether the Red Talons bring them into it or not, on account of teetering on the brink and frequently being the targets of both directed enemy action and human issues..</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but there's a big difference between being in the conflict because you are endangered and being in the conflict because you run with the Werewolves and live in or around their Caerns.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-23T12:02:55-07:00","id":919848,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=919757#post919757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">From what I understand, the alpha wolf phenomenon is a product of artificially formed wolf packs. In the wild, wolf packs are actually family units and don't have a hierarchy so much as they have a mother, a father, and a bunch of cubs of various ages. There really isn't much in the way of challenging the alpha for control of the pack, it would be pretty bad for genetic diversity.</div> </div> </div> </div>You are going way too much the other way. A family with parents and children still exist in a hierarchy. Even when the cubs grow up and are full adults, the dad is still in charge. It's still a hierarchical relationship. And when the father wolf gets too old and can't hack it anymore, one of his sons takes over because firm leadership is what's needed for survival. Dad then either accepts his demotion, or goes off on his own.<br /> <br /> The early wolf studies are still valid, but the researchers learned it worked differently when talking about family units rather than unrelated wolves. But much of the basics are still there. A pack leader is an alpha, even if it is important to understand he's alpha because he's the father or some other patriarchal figure (like an uncle). In a family relationship, conflict is more subdued than with unrelated wolves. But many of the roles and behavior are still there.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Given that, you may have just brought forward one of the biggest points of conflict between the Red Talons and their Patron. In general, Werewolves can't fill traditional obligations for either human or wolf society. Mating for life implies a lot more than just a quick action then years of abandonment. Perhaps Griffin's charge is something the Red Talons can't maintain, so on some level he is always angry with them, which in turn stokes the fury of the tribe as a whole. On the flip side, maybe the Red Talons actually do find mates and bond with them and bring them into their own packs. In a strange turnabout it could be the Red Talons who keep the biggest ties to the mortal world by bringing their families into the conflict. </div> </div> </div>I don't think mate for life is a ban from Griffin. I think Griffin understands clearly griffins are griffins, wolves are wolves, and Garou are Garou. However, I do think Garou respect and often try to emulate the behavior of their totems. I like the comparison to monks. Not all Red Talons choose to be super chaste, but there may be a minority who do, and many Red Talons may look up to them for taking on this additional ban even if it is not something they'd do themselves.<br /> <br /> My thoughts are not that &quot;all Red Talons are this way&quot;. It is that some may be. I like options for PCs, and as an ST I want some nice flavor text ways I can show how this tribe is a little bit different than the others.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't hold that Griffin is angry with the Talons if they don't emulate him perfectly, but it is something I think an ST could have fun with in their game. I'd just want to be sure there was some kind of benefit and approval from their tribe's totem because otherwise it becomes a very unhealthy relationship.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-23T12:46:22-07:00","id":919874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=919757#post919757\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In general, Werewolves can't fill traditional obligations for either human or wolf society. Mating for life implies a lot more than just a quick action then years of abandonment.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is true, and the Curse definitely affects animals as well as humans. It is said that the Curse affects kinfolk less than non-kinfolk, but it is kept vague (purposefully in my opinion). Still, this would present a challenge to being loyal to one's mate. Furthermore, if the Red Talon reproduces - and they will - then it is very important for the mated pair to have an effective pack around them as the pack overall is responsible for the cubs. One reason why it is usually only the alpha pair that produces children is that other pairs do not have the resources to raise their children successfully.<br /> <br /> Wolves live up to 20 years, although an 8 year life span is more typical. I wonder if part of a Red Talon's life is that st some point he is given 8-12 years off to live among a real wolf pack, find a mate, reproduce, and then watch over the pack as its alpha. If the cubs produce a Garou, then after the First Change, the parent makes sure it is dropped off at the local caern. Even for general lupus, they probably need 2-4 wars to reproduce and make sure the cubs reach maturity.<br /> <br /> This is different than the homids. Homids can abandon their young because they can instruct kinfolk to watch over them, and in some cases cuckolda a man so he raises the child as one of his own. This really can't be done with wolves. The parent has to be there with the pack to make sure the cubs receive enough food, otherwise it risks some other mated pair getting the resources for their cubs and their starve. The cubs could survive without their Garou parent, but it is much more of a risk than with humans.<br /> <br /> Low rage Garou would have the most success to live with a wolf pack, so this probably happens when the Garou is quite young and less likely to have increased their Rage. Of course, Ahrouns and Galliards would still have lots of problems. We can imagine maybe their pack is just them and a mated female plus any children, and they leave the pack more quickly than other auspices. Perhaps it is these auspices most likely to go &quot;monk&quot; and just have the one mate for life. While the lower Rage Red Talons may go the &quot;family route&quot; and take multiple breaks throughout their life to run with the wolf packs. <br /> <br /> Perhaps this is an important function among the Ragabash - they are expected to father most of the children among the tribe and spend the most time than others fathering/mothering the next generation of kinfolk and Garou. Since Ragabash are the &quot;questioners of the ways&quot;, this serial monogamy in defiance of Griffin's nature of mating for life is less offensive to Griffin than it would if done by the other auspices. If so, this makes Red Talon Ragabash extremely important for the survival of the tribe.<br /> <br /> This is a very different dynamic than what typically occurs with homid Garou. This has been an interesting digression.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-24T06:14:48-07:00","id":920318,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=919848#post919848\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You are going way too much the other way. A family with parents and children still exist in a hierarchy. Even when the cubs grow up and are full adults, the dad is still in charge. It's still a hierarchical relationship. And when the father wolf gets too old and can't hack it anymore, one of his sons takes over because firm leadership is what's needed for survival. Dad then either accepts his demotion, or goes off on his own.<br /> <br /> The early wolf studies are still valid, but the researchers learned it worked differently when talking about family units rather than unrelated wolves. But much of the basics are still there. A pack leader is an alpha, even if it is important to understand he's alpha because he's the father or some other patriarchal figure (like an uncle). In a family relationship, conflict is more subdued than with unrelated wolves. But many of the roles and behavior are still there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh yeah, dominance behavior is still fundamentally part of wolf, it's the only presumption of strict hierarchical ranks that's been called into question. Many of the behaviors formerly associated with establishing rank can also be the precursor to driving adult wolves out to form packs of their own, and the non-alpha's don't breed because everyone around them is a blood relation. That's also why challenging for leadership isn't what we originally thought, a young wolf can't take the position of father in a pack where all the females are his mother and sisters.<br /> <br /> In any event, I was bringing it up to point out that fulfilling the role of 'alpha' in a wolf pack was about much more than just being the biggest and the strongest and keeping the other wolves from mating.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=919848#post919848\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't think mate for life is a ban from Griffin. I think Griffin understands clearly griffins are griffins, wolves are wolves, and Garou are Garou. However, I do think Garou respect and often try to emulate the behavior of their totems. I like the comparison to monks. Not all Red Talons choose to be super chaste, but there may be a minority who do, and many Red Talons may look up to them for taking on this additional ban even if it is not something they'd do themselves.<br /> <br /> My thoughts are not that &quot;all Red Talons are this way&quot;. It is that some may be. I like options for PCs, and as an ST I want some nice flavor text ways I can show how this tribe is a little bit different than the others.<br /> <br /> I wouldn't hold that Griffin is angry with the Talons if they don't emulate him perfectly, but it is something I think an ST could have fun with in their game. I'd just want to be sure there was some kind of benefit and approval from their tribe's totem because otherwise it becomes a very unhealthy relationship.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's fair. I tend to like to play up that tribal totems are a burden as well as a boon, and that sometimes they thunder for what cannot be easily given, so that's the direction I tend to approach things from. It's interesting to see the different ways a tribe might take to fulfill the unfulfillable.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=919874#post919874\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps this is an important function among the Ragabash - they are expected to father most of the children among the tribe and spend the most time than others fathering/mothering the next generation of kinfolk and Garou. Since Ragabash are the &quot;questioners of the ways&quot;, this serial monogamy in defiance of Griffin's nature of mating for life is less offensive to Griffin than it would if done by the other auspices. If so, this makes Red Talon Ragabash extremely important for the survival of the tribe.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is an interesting way of taking things. It also means the Ragabash of the Red Talons are going to have a corresponding reputation, perhaps of being older and more reserved and more capable of leadership than those of most other tribes. Obviously leading a wolf pack does not always make one fit for leading a werewolf pack, but it might work out that Ragabash end up in leadership positions more often in the Red Talons than they do in other tribes.<br /> <br /> Another (likely Ragabash phenomena) would be nursery packs that raise the cubs for Talons with too much rage to do it themselves. Not as ideal as the parents raising cubs themselves, but it fits Griffin in other ways.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-24T18:10:18-07:00","id":920670,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=920318#post920318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Another (likely Ragabash phenomena) would be nursery packs that raise the cubs for Talons with too much rage to do it themselves. Not as ideal as the parents raising cubs themselves, but it fits Griffin in other ways.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's an interesting idea I hadn't considered.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-26T17:25:35-07:00","id":921771,"title":"","content":"This post is not really about Griffin, but one of the camps of the Red Talons. It incorporates some of the idea previously mentioned to better develop the camp.<br /> <br /> <b>Lodge of the Predator Kings</b><br /> <br /> As written in the original tribebook, it was the Lodge \u2013 not the tribe as a whole \u2013 who were dedicated to the return of the Imperigum and the complete eradication of humanity. However, this distinction was lost in the corebooks who described the entire tribe seeking genocide. Future supplements then described an \u201canti-extermination faction\u201d. The tribebook then broke this down into three camps representing two extremes and a \u201cmoderate\u201d faction which hated humanity, but did not push extermination.<br /> <br /> The \u201cextermination\u201d camp does not have much to interest me except a rather peculiar name. What is with the name? The Talons aren't known for being poetical, but rather straight forward. Why are they a \u201clodge\u201d (a very human concept if you think about it) and who are the \u201cpredator kings\u201d? Are the kings the Red Talon members of the lodge, or something else?<br /> <br /> A lodge is merely a small building. A man made structure would hold no appeal to the Red Talons. However, lodge is often the name for a local order of a secret or fraternal society. This must be the reason for the camps' name. So what kind of secret sor fraternal society is the Lodge of the Predator Kings? We know the purpose includes the extermination of mankind. But is there something more?<br /> <br /> The first thing that springs to my mind is that the Lodge sounds very similar to native American societies like the Ghost Dance. The Ghost Dancers were formed when then Indians were obviously losing to the white man, and their way of life and sovereignty were coming to an end. The secret society believed they had a way in which they could reverse their defeats and restore the world as they once knew it. This sounds exactly like the purpose of the Lodge of the Predator Kings. Such societies are not rare \u2013 they often occur in cultures under threat. The Righteous and Harmonious Fists, popularly known as the Boxers, in China was similar. The South African Xhosa began killing their cattle, under the influence of a prophetess named Nongqawuse, because they believed it would drive out the British. All believed they could summon s upernatural powers that would eliminate their enemies.<br /> <br /> Such thinking is common among apocalyptic religious sects. An apocalypse is actually a \u201ca vision of heavenly secrets that can make sense of earthly realities.&quot; It often involves the end of the world, but at its heart is an explanation of why the faithful are persecuted right now, and promises that the oppressors and other evil doers will be eventually be destroyed. The ties to the general themes of Werewolf and the position of the Red Talons should be obvious.<br /> <br /> The Lodge therefore is an apoclyptic sect. Lodge members believe they have received divine visions related to the apocalypse, and they are acting upon these visions in order to prepare for the Apocalypse. As this camp is exclusive to the Red Talons, I believe these visions must be associated with Griffin.<br /> <br /> These are not Garou with a realistic plan to bring about the end of humanity. They are idealists clutching at straws in order to fight off depair. In many ways, membership could be a way to fight off Harano. The one advantage they have is that in the World of Darkness, supernatural powers are very real so they aren't entirely delusional.<br /> <br /> The Ghost Dancers believed they were reunited with the spirits of the dead, and could bring these spirits of the dead to fight to the living world and fight on their behalf. Therefore, I believe the Red Talons of the Lodge are in commune with the spirits of the lost \u201cpredator kings\u201d - the saberetooth tiger, dire wolf, terror birds, cave lion, bear dogs (Amphicyonidae), and other carnivores that became extinct during the Pleistocene or early Holocene (all most likely driven to extinction by mankind, but this is not certain).<br /> <br /> These spirits of lost animals all seem to be part of the brood of Griffin so there is an existing connection between them and the Red Talons. The Lodge is in communion with them, hoping to rally the last ebbing powers of the spirits \u2013 and their anger against humanity \u2013 to destroy mankind and recreate the world back to what it was before. The best way to reach such spirits is to travel to the Near Realm of Pangaea, and it is likely the Lodge has a \u201chome\u201d there where the leaders of the Lodge go.<br /> <br /> Therefore, the Lodge of the Predator Kings is primarily a religious movement, not a warrior society. Its positions possibly violate several taboos (as did the Ghost Dancers) among the Red Talons which explains why it is a minority movement. Many Red Talons might respect and admire the Lodge and its members for reminding the other Garou they remain proud, but probably think it is just too extreme and not actually helpful.<br /> <br /> The Lodge probably spends most of their time talking to the spirits, training members, and preparing for the great day when the Predator Kings return and lead the Tribe to victory during the Apocalypse. Their \u201cwar\u201d is not to be fought now. What direct action they take is probably limited, seem more as a training exercise instead of a battle, and intended more to keep morale up than an operation intended to lead to victory. They will however target specific violators of Gaia for destruction, perhaps as a result of local interaction with humans or as signs given to them by the spirits.<br /> <br /> They are still the most bloodthirsty camp of the Red Talons. Unlike the Warders, they still go out of their way to kill humans. They do believe one day humanity will be extinct, but that will be the result of the apocalypse and the new world they inherit will be cleansed of mankind and the lost predator kings will return. However, the camp now has other aspects which should deepen roleplay and give its members more of a three dimensional feel even if they remain villains in a chronicle.<br /> <br /> I think this makes the camp and tribe much more playable while retaining its main characteristics.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-28T06:24:02-07:00","id":922574,"title":"","content":"First off I want to say that this is a very interesting look into making the Red Talons usable as something besides easy villains and psychopathic human haters. I have always had a problem with the Red Talons in that I hate having to hit groups with the NPC hammer, but the Red Talons have always been hard for me not to, given they're less then spectacular presentation in the books. Whenever I have used Red Talons I always used them as focuses for Wyld and wild. Compared to it's polar opposite the Glass Walkers, they have been spontaneous, had constantly shifting power structures, and an aptitude for understanding the mercurial nature of spirits. Until I read this the only ways I had to use them were as villains or reclusive hermit theurges. This look at Gryffon has given me a lot of ideas on how to include Red Talons in my games, and give PC's options for them. So thank you for doing this and I would love it if you did this for others if not all of the tribal totems.<br /> <br /> P.S. I know this post may not have added much information or content to the thread, but given my username, I feel It would have been amiss for me not to say anything at all, so as an the fifteenth advocate of Griffin, I approve of this thread <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-29T20:50:21-07:00","id":923679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=922574#post922574\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So thank you for doing this and I would love it if you did this for others if not all of the tribal totems.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thanks for your kind words and support. I'm glad you can use something. I do like it that others post, even if it disagrees with me or develops an idea that I left out there. Even the posts that say &quot;I like this specific idea&quot; let's me know which ideas are more appealing.<br /> <br /> I know other people have asked if I could do all the tribal totems. Not sure I am up to that. Technically I've done three (Rat and Unicorn beside this) and I have some ideas for a few more, but I don't have as many &quot;heretical thoughts&quot; about the other totems. <br /> <br /> When I posted stuff on Unicorn and Griffin, I had two big incentives. One was that neither tribal totem seemed to be well defined according to the actual legends about the creatures. Most of the totems are real animals so it was easy to pull from what we knew of them. The fantasy totems though needed to be based more on the legends, but they they didn't reflect them well. The other was that the Children of Gaia and Red Talons seem to be the most problematic tribes. People often post they either dislike them or at least disliked the tribe book. They needed the most revision to make the playable as PCs or interesting as NPCs. Many people seem to want to like them, but were unenthusiastic about canonical portrayals. So I knew there was a void there waiting to be filled.<br /> <br /> The other tribes don't have that problem, or at least not as severely. While I do have some different ideas, I think the threads would be more about &quot;this is what I don't like about this tribe, so this is what I've done with them&quot;. I don't think that's as beneficial to others. If I can come up with an interesting hook (to me) though, I may do it. When I posted about Rat, it wasn't about things from legends on Rat, but something I felt the tribe's canonical depiction pointed to, but which never developed. So it was a few more plot hooks about the Bone Gnawers as opposed to a re-evaluation about Rat.<br /> <br /> But given the feedback, I'll probably post about another totem after I'm done with this. I still have a few ideas though, as well as posing some questions to the community to flesh out my ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-30T20:25:26-07:00","id":924264,"title":"","content":"<b>Griffin as a Predator and Totem of War</b><br /> <br /> This post is not about a new perspective on Griffin based on myth and legend. But examination of why Griffin is a totem of war, and what this tells us. Griffin is a war totem that rejects homids and the ways of mankind. It it s a \u201cwar\u201d spirit for lupus. From an animal perspective, \u201cwar\u201d would mean hunting. I think we need to keep this in mind when we hear people (whether it be homid Garou or the human writers of the game books) describe the Red Talons as seeking \u201cwar\u201d with mankind.<br /> <br /> Do you mean war as mankind understands the term? Or how an animal would understand it?<br /> <br /> I think the Red Talons should approach their war with mankind in the same manner that wolves function as predators in a Darwinian sense. The role of predators is to winnow out the weak and sick so that their prey becomes strong.<br /> <br /> A good example of this is an eskimo folktale contained in the novel Never Cry Wolf. It is a folktale about the caribou and wolf, and it ends as \u201cthis is why the caribou and the wolf are one; for the caribou feeds the wolf, but it is the wolf who keeps the caribou strong.&quot;<br /> <br /> This is the <u>sacred relationship</u> between predator and prey that is honored in the minor Rite of Prayer for the Prey. In werewolf, Griffin has the role of the king or leader of the predator spirits. So this should be an important element in his worship and relation to the Garou. It should inform how the Red Talons act.<br /> <br /> This gives us a different perspective of how the Red Talons might imagine their war with mankind. At least, this is probably how Red Talons viewed it until the past few centuries.<br /> <br /> Of course the big problem is that man is not supposed to be prey. Nor do I think the Red Talons really reject that. The Litany forbids eating the flesh of man. And it is clear that Man is a very different animal than the everyone else. At the same time, the Red Talons realize man is mortal and is not god. So while man is not prey, it is still through the lens of hunting that the Red Talons conceive of their war.<br /> <br /> They see that Humanity became corrupt because predators did not play the role with man that they played for all other species. Predation kept sickness, weakness, and other forms of corruption out of the other animals. They didn't do it for man though, and that is why mankind acts how he does. He is filled with the weakness, sickness, corruption, etc. that the Garou should eliminated from him.<br /> <br /> Now, in reality predators don't just go after the weak and sick. They kill anything they can at the time so they can eat. However, more often than not, what they kill is not the strongest and healthiest, but the least so. Over time, the weak and sick are winnowed and the prey become stronger.<br /> <br /> While wolves are not aware enough to know this, the Red Talons will be especially \u2013 especially if we believe this symbiotic relationship is embodied in the spirit world.<br /> <br /> Therefore, the important element/strategy in the Red Talons' war with mankind is to perform this predation function. Don't go after the \u201chealthy\u201d humans who display the characteristics that a healthy species shows. Go after the \u201csick\u201d humans who show sign of corruption. Of course, this \u201csickness\u201d includes \u201cspiritual sickness\u201d. The evidence for such sickeness would be the humans responsible for creating the pollution they hate, the development of resorts in previous pristine wilderness, and the other kind of plots STs will routinely use for their antagonists.<br /> <br /> Even if the Red Talons consider all humans to be diseased to some extent, their proper role is to not target the \u201chealthiest\u201d humans, but the most diseased. Of course, the most diseased humans live in the scabs far away from Red Talon protectorates so true opportunities for their hunt is limited. But this attitude will be the framework how they handle any human settlements in or near their protectorates, and may be the basis of some Red Talon diplomacy with septs closer to human civilization.<br /> <br /> Certain human populations might be totally safe from Red Talon predation this way. In the books, Red Talons seem to get along the Wendigo. Their kinfolk and humans who look like them, or at least in areas part of the Wendigo protectorate, might be excluded from their general war. Perhaps this is an important function of the \u201cWhelps Compromise\u201d - go to urban septs and identify these targets that maybe the Urrah aren't getting rid of. They may leave the rest of humanity alone, but they do bring back some good intel to keep good ties with the rest of the tribe.<br /> <br /> So while in the books, the Red Talons really don't discriminate who they call among humans, it might be better if there was a method to which they adhered, especially when they are proactive about waging the war instead of reactive to human incursions in their territories. If we introduce the idea that the culling of the herd is not to be indiscriminate, then it shows the Red Talons are thinking and using their brains. It also makes them more like hunters or warriors than psychopaths. It might even be interesting to see the Red Talons decide to NOT kill a human because it isn't the right kind of human to kill, it could even shock the PCs. At least it introduces a means to better include Red Talons into a chronicle.<br /> <br /> Ultimately, the Red Talons believe that Man will go extinct in the apocalypse so their current policy may not advocate that now (of course, this does not mean there aren't some Red Talons who feel this way, but they still need to figure out a plan on how to do this and convince the other Red Talons they can win. The rest of the tribe may be sympathetic, but still be \u201crealist\u201d and look forward to the day that will come). But that doesn't mean they won't kill humans. They do, and sometimes this will be done proactively. I'm just trying to put it in context. What the Talons mean by \u201cwar\u201d, and what the homids THINK the Talons mean may be different things.<br /> <br /> In any event, that the Red Talons have a \u201cdeeper meaning\u201d to their war on humans and that the Impergium had a spiritual significance and important function that \u201cbenefited\u201d Man would be something that could provoke debate or at least a good roleplayed scene that PCs might not expect when talking to a Red Talon.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-03T23:16:54-07:00","id":925746,"title":"","content":"<b>Deep History of the Tribe</b><br /> <br /> I've reached the point where I really don't have new ideas to add, but I still have things I'd like to explore. One is that for the vast majority of the history of the tribe, humanity isn't actually all that threatening. Even after the Impergium there is a good one to two thousand years before any significant amount of wilderness is lost to cultivation. So we have about 6000-8000 of the tribe where their modern focus has no reason at all. Red Talons may not like humans, but I don't think there'd be anything like their hatred nowadays.<br /> <br /> So what do people think the Red Talons do in this era? Remember that for most of this time, there is no Gauntlet so the Realm and Penumbra are one. And that physical creatures like unicorns, griffins, and others are actually present.<br /> <br /> Another question is how do the Red Talons interact with the Fae in this era? Most of this time is before the Sundering. Fae are not animals, but they are definitely not humans. Do they get along well with them and other chimerical creatures? Might they more frequently assume homid form in order to interact with them?<br /> <br /> I'm interested in this as I see the Red Talons being one of the tribes with very strong ties with their Ancestors. And for most of their Ancestors, this is the time they lived in - a time when humans were not a threat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T08:01:36-07:00","id":925896,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=925746#post925746\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Deep History of the Tribe</b><br /> <br /> I've reached the point where I really don't have new ideas to add, but I still have things I'd like to explore. One is that for the vast majority of the history of the tribe, humanity isn't actually all that threatening. Even after the Impergium there is a good one to two thousand years before any significant amount of wilderness is lost to cultivation. So we have about 6000-8000 of the tribe where their modern focus has no reason at all. Red Talons may not like humans, but I don't think there'd be anything like their hatred nowadays.<br /> <br /> So what do people think the Red Talons do in this era? Remember that for most of this time, there is no Gauntlet so the Realm and Penumbra are one. And that physical creatures like unicorns, griffins, and others are actually present.<br /> <br /> Another question is how do the Red Talons interact with the Fae in this era? Most of this time is before the Sundering. Fae are not animals, but they are definitely not humans. Do they get along well with them and other chimerical creatures? Might they more frequently assume homid form in order to interact with them?<br /> <br /> I'm interested in this as I see the Red Talons being one of the tribes with very strong ties with their Ancestors. And for most of their Ancestors, this is the time they lived in - a time when humans were not a threat.</div> </div> </div> </div>Domestication of the dog from the wolf occurred a few tens of thousands of years ago (at least 27), and what resulted could no longer become Garou. So there is that, and it seems like a major sticking point on the order of &quot;we must never let humans be in charge of wolves&quot;. The not-yet-Red Talons would likely act to keep wolves away from human kennels to keep any further domestication from occurring, which would largely mean killing humans that tried to do selective breeding whenever they found out about it, particularly later on, when domestication was kicking into higher gear. As humans started domesticating other animals after the Impergium ended, the not-quite-yet-Talons would likely see this as a sign that relenting in the Impergium was a mistake.<br /> <br /> As for the fae, most of the Talons would probably see them as very troublesome spirits. A few that take on beast shapes might be more relatable, up until they engage in some strange bans and use &quot;weird&quot; magic. So, at best, it would be an approach with caution. Any fae that speaks well of domestication or humans can expect a fight with the proto-Talons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T16:31:37-07:00","id":926146,"title":"","content":"While there is some debate about precisely when dogs were domesticated, the time I use is around 15,000 years ago. I think all the data best supports that. I also use the date of 12,000 years ago (the last Glacial Maximum when the Pleistocene became the Holocene) as the time when the Garou and other Changing Breeds happened. That's never been established as canon and know others prefer a different date, but it makes sense to me. Based on DNA analysis, the domestication of dogs appear to happen only once. So there weren't multiple domestication of wolves into dogs. Even the dogs of the American Indians came from the original dogs of Eurasia. Of course, in the World of Darkness, maybe that's because the Red Talons prevented any more from occurring.<br /> <br /> The timeline of when domestication occurred is the following.<br /> <br /> Around 12,000 BP (Before Present) - goats, sheep, pigs<br /> <br /> 10,000 BP - cattle, cats<br /> <br /> 8,000 BP - chicken<br /> <br /> 7,000 BP - donkey<br /> <br /> 6,000 BP - honeybee<br /> <br /> 5,000 BP - horse<br /> <br /> During this time period, the human population went from around 1 million worldwide to 5 million. Very small numbers. I hold the Impergium lasting from around 8000-5000 BP. After 5000 BP, the human population growth begins to accelerate. Some people may want to push the Impergium back a bit. Most domestication occurred before agriculture took hold. Herding was established long before agriculture. Certainly the most important food animals were before the Impergium.<br /> <br /> I don't think at this time period the Red Talons were much focused on humanity. They were probably the most negative Garou tribe towards Man, but there is not much Glory, Honor, or Wisdom savaging more or less helpless humans. The Garou of this period have to be fighting the elder evils of this time period - Wyrm-corrupted forces of the Wyld like giants and dragons or more alien horrors. The Garou also made up a much larger percentage of humanity than they are today, and there probably was no Veil. So a much higher percentage of humans &quot;belonged&quot; to one of the existing tribes, and they wouldn't like it if the Red Talons preyed on them actively.<br /> <br /> So while the Red Talons may be hostile towards Man, I don't think it should be their defining trait in this time period. Man would be an irritant, not a threat. I'm looking for an alternate &quot;hook&quot; for the tribe during this time period. There's not much to do on the animal side of things, which is why I'm bringing up things like the Fae or other mythic beings the Red Talons would interact with or focus on as antagonists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T19:03:00-07:00","id":926206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=926146#post926146\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While there is some debate about precisely when dogs were domesticated, the time I use is around 15,000 years ago. I think all the data best supports that. I also use the date of 12,000 years ago (the last Glacial Maximum when the Pleistocene became the Holocene) as the time when the Garou and other Changing Breeds happened. That's never been established as canon and know others prefer a different date, but it makes sense to me. Based on DNA analysis, the domestication of dogs appear to happen only once. So there weren't multiple domestication of wolves into dogs. Even the dogs of the American Indians came from the original dogs of Eurasia. Of course, in the World of Darkness, maybe that's because the Red Talons prevented any more from occurring.<br /> <br /> The timeline of when domestication occurred is the following.<br /> <br /> Around 12,000 BP (Before Present) - goats, sheep, pigs</div> </div> </div> </div>Around the start of the War of Rage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 10,000 BP - cattle, cats<br /> <br /> 8,000 BP - chicken </div> </div> </div>Late to post-WoR<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <br /> During this time period, the human population went from around 1 million worldwide to 5 million. Very small numbers. I hold the Impergium lasting from around 8000-5000 BP. </div> </div> </div>That's not supported by the text. The Impergium was over and done before cities like Catalhuyuk became a thing, and it started at an indeterminate amount of time after the Garou came into being, likely one the order of tens of thousands of years ago..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-04T20:06:53-07:00","id":926224,"title":"","content":"We're obviously in disagreement with a lot of timing. The Impergium is supposed to be based on the agricultural revolution, that is the earliest around 12,000 to 10,000 BP. I place it later than that because otherwise the Impergium is limited to a handful of tribes and only in the Fertile Crescent region instead of it affecting all tribes and being worldwide. By pushing it forward to around 8000 BP, it means agriculture has spread to China, Europe, and Ethiopia and all the tribes can be involved. Prior to that, it means the local Garou of the Fertile Crescent might be perplexed at what the local humans are doing, but it isn't until all the Tribes see its spread to their regions that the first concolations are held to start the Impergium.<br /> <br /> The idea that the War of Rage is around 12,000 to 8,000 BP means the Impergium happened before agriculture during the Ice Age. That doesn't make sense to me. At the most, the Impergium might be around 10,000 to 7,000 BP. But if it does, then it essentially ends before agriculture becomes widespread and many tribes are not involved.<br /> <br /> In terms of actual text in the books, the timeline of the Impergium has been all over the place. However, they tend to be any 3000 year period between 12,000-4,000 BP.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-05T06:48:28-07:00","id":926379,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=926224#post926224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're obviously in disagreement with a lot of timing. The Impergium is supposed to be based on the agricultural revolution, that is the earliest around 12,000 to 10,000 BP. I place it later than that because otherwise the Impergium is limited to a handful of tribes and only in the Fertile Crescent region instead of it affecting all tribes and being worldwide. By pushing it forward to around 8000 BP, it means agriculture has spread to China, Europe, and Ethiopia and all the tribes can be involved. Prior to that, it means the local Garou of the Fertile Crescent might be perplexed at what the local humans are doing, but it isn't until all the Tribes see its spread to their regions that the first concolations are held to start the Impergium.<br /> <br /> The idea that the War of Rage is around 12,000 to 8,000 BP means the Impergium happened before agriculture during the Ice Age. That doesn't make sense to me. At the most, the Impergium might be around 10,000 to 7,000 BP. But if it does, then it essentially ends before agriculture becomes widespread and many tribes are not involved.<br /> <br /> In terms of actual text in the books, the timeline of the Impergium has been all over the place. However, they tend to be any 3000 year period between 12,000-4,000 BP.</div> </div> </div> </div>War of Rage has been 12 to 9 kya since about second edition. First edition placed everything far later, but only had it lasting for 1000 years (3000-2000 BCE), which is a rather scanty stretch of time to leave the kind of scars that inflict the Delirium in all humans, and would leave a lot of groups out, making the Delirium far less widespread; in order to get a universal reaction in all humans, the events would have to have started before humans spread around, which would have given a start date of 100 kya or so, which is when you had migrations out of Africa.<br /> <br /> Given that the Weaver is often presented as coming to humans to win them over in a bizarre juxtaposition reminiscent of the serpent in the Garden of Eden, the Garou would have been making sure that none of that took root. There were no tribes or tribal totems at the time; those didn't come until after the Impergium was over and done with for generations, Red Talons included.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-05T13:42:14-07:00","id":926605,"title":"","content":"I think you may be incorporating some personal canon with your timeline. I certainly haven't read all the Werewolf books out there, but nothing I remember says the War of Rage took place 12,000-9000 BP.<br /> <br /> The most definite answer I can find in the books I have access to is that the Concord happened 9000 years ago which means the Impergium happened 12,000-9,000 BP. I've explained why I don't use that time period (if the Impergium ended in 7000 BC, then it ended before agriculture was introduced in Europe and China), but certainly it's supported by canon (given how nebulous when every other event happens, I think the 7000 BC figure is not firm, but just a guideline to show this happened in the early Neolithic). <br /> <br /> But the War of Rage can't be happening then unless you hold it happened while the Impergium is going on. Usually, the history we're given says it happened after the Impergium, but this is contradicted in other places when people says that it might have happened before and during the Impergium. Things are very, very loose here.<br /> <br /> So while you're free to use that timeline in your own games and be happy that it's not necessarily contradicted by canon, others can hold it at a different time and also be happy that they can find things in canon to support them. I don't think either of us are wrong or right - the books can be used to support either of our timelines. It's just personal preference.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure how much of this has any relevance to the Red Talons. It's just that any hostility the Talons had against man's domestication of animals isn't a post-Impergium function. The most important herding animals were domesticated either before the Impergium or while it was being done.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-05T19:56:26-07:00","id":926791,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=926605#post926605\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you may be incorporating some personal canon with your timeline. I certainly haven't read all the Werewolf books out there, but nothing I remember says the War of Rage took place 12,000-9000 BP.<br /> <br /> The most definite answer I can find in the books I have access to is that the Concord happened 9000 years ago which means the Impergium happened 12,000-9,000 BP. I've explained why I don't use that time period (if the Impergium ended in 7000 BC, then it ended before agriculture was introduced in Europe and China), but certainly it's supported by canon (given how nebulous when every other event happens, I think the 7000 BC figure is not firm, but just a guideline to show this happened in the early Neolithic).</div> </div> </div> </div>First edition's Mnesis breakdown actually gave 12 kya as the time of the Impergium. The Revised Player's Guide to Garou mentioned that it lasted for more than three millennia, and the Revised ST Handbook mentions that it officially ended as an official kind of practice before humans began building cities (presumably ones that were on record and lasted for some time, since that section starts with talk of ancient cities growing out of river valleys worldwide and Garou wiping nascent cultures from history). So, that's where I got the years from. Also notable, some mini-Impergiums might have been practiced later on, much as with flare-ups of conflicts between Garou and Fera.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the War of Rage can't be happening then unless you hold it happened while the Impergium is going on. Usually, the history we're given says it happened after the Impergium, but this is contradicted in other places when people says that it might have happened before and during the Impergium. Things are very, very loose here.<br /> <br /> So while you're free to use that timeline in your own games and be happy that it's not necessarily contradicted by canon, others can hold it at a different time and also be happy that they can find things in canon to support them. I don't think either of us are wrong or right - the books can be used to support either of our timelines. It's just personal preference.<br /> <br /> I'm not sure how much of this has any relevance to the Red Talons. It's just that any hostility the Talons had against man's domestication of animals isn't a post-Impergium function. The most important herding animals were domesticated either before the Impergium or while it was being done. </div> </div> </div>Well, the note about Garou removing human cultures from history? That's presumably what the Red Talons did more than a few times, and formed their specialty during their era as human exterminators. They weren't claiming human kin, so the Talons or the group that would later become them probably saw their errands as purer than the rest, without conflicts of interest from being family with a group of humans. Some might have even attacked said kin, to try and loose other Garou from their attachments to humanity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T02:47:48-07:00","id":928029,"title":"","content":"Shattered Dreams gives a different timeline with the wars of rage being mroe a series of on and off conflicts than one continuous thing. It also has larger prototribes that modern tribes broke off of.. so the answer for what the Red Talons were doing between the end of the Imperigum and man becoming a serious issue was... they didn't exist as a seperate group?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T10:52:02-07:00","id":928261,"title":"","content":"Granted Shattered Dreams also talks about a conflict between homid and lupus garou as well....how could that play into all this as well?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T16:12:05-07:00","id":928488,"title":"","content":"I haven't read Shattered Dreams so feel free to elaborate on any ideas it have that may be of interest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T16:24:39-07:00","id":928496,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=926791#post926791\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, the note about Garou removing human cultures from history? That's presumably what the Red Talons did more than a few times, and formed their specialty during their era as human exterminators. They weren't claiming human kin, so the Talons or the group that would later become them probably saw their errands as purer than the rest, without conflicts of interest from being family with a group of humans. Some might have even attacked said kin, to try and loose other Garou from their attachments to humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's actually something I'd like to see the tribe get away from during the very early days. I don't mind them killing humans and even being ruthless about it if they trespass Red Talon territory, or violate certain taboos. But I don't like an &quot;extermination&quot; angle for the tribe so far in the past. That just reinforces the stereotypical image.<br /> <br /> If we go over 12,000 years of history, really it's only been in the last 500-2000 that mankind began throwing off the normal Malthusian limits of the carrying capacity. Before that time, civilization was very limited, and &quot;barbarianism&quot; was the rule with very strict limits on human population. It is a very different set of circumstances Red Talons find themselves against humanity than nowadays. Now, the Red Talons are one of the minor tribes, and they have very little ability to deter/defeat mankind. In say 2000 BC, the Red Talons would be a major tribe (perhaps even a contending leadership tribe among the lupus), and would be in the position to dominate whatever human population existed among them locally.<br /> <br /> In other words, while the Red Talons would still be hostile to humans, I don't think they'd be particularly concerned about humans. I think they should be focusing on something else. Until 3000 BC or so, not even the great river valley agricultural civilizations are developed enough to worry many people.<br /> <br /> Today's Talons are really obsessed about humanity and their plight as a result of it. But that was not always the case. So in going back to these distant times, I'm wondering what other things were the Talons possibly interested in. Once we know that, then we can see if any of it may be of us for us running the tribe today.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T16:35:26-07:00","id":928500,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=928488#post928488\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I haven't read Shattered Dreams so feel free to elaborate on any ideas it have that may be of interest.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well its in the write up they had (as the pdf isn't out yet). But they explain that there were tons of wars that aren't getting covered in the book like wars between the bastet (I had one in my cannon with the Pumanca vs the Balam because I wanted the Pumanca as a tribe isolated from the Balam and to also use the war as a reason that the Jaguars of the US died out....) but also between Homid and Lupus Garou. Basically the Lupus garou saw the homids as not true Garou and weak, so initially they hunted and killed them (part of the impergium as a justification?), but over time as humans became more advanced the homids began to get the upper hand in the conflict and it fell by the wayside. They don't dedicate a lot of words to it, which I personally consider tragic as it has a lot of potential. I didn't think about it until Shattered Dreams was brought up in this thread. Any thoughts on this with your version of the Red Talons? Would that change anything to you or just give you more to work with? I've been reading your posts and will be stealing some and leaving some behind for my own games (though your bit on Rat screamed to me, because i'd been thinking the same thing for years.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1179-celtspz","name":"CeltSPZ","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1179_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"835"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T16:58:35-07:00","id":928511,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=928496#post928496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's actually something I'd like to see the tribe get away from during the very early days. I don't mind them killing humans and even being ruthless about it if they trespass Red Talon territory, or violate certain taboos. But I don't like an &quot;extermination&quot; angle for the tribe so far in the past. That just reinforces the stereotypical image.</div> </div> </div> </div>Extermination was carried out by all werewolves. The ones who would become the Red Talons would have just been more open about what they would have considered targets. Homid Garou live there? Sucks to be their kin.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If we go over 12,000 years of history, really it's only been in the last 500-2000 that mankind began throwing off the normal Malthusian limits of the carrying capacity. Before that time, civilization was very limited, and &quot;barbarianism&quot; was the rule with very strict limits on human population. It is a very different set of circumstances Red Talons find themselves against humanity than nowadays. Now, the Red Talons are one of the minor tribes, and they have very little ability to deter/defeat mankind. In say 2000 BC, the Red Talons would be a major tribe (perhaps even a contending leadership tribe among the lupus), and would be in the position to dominate whatever human population existed among them locally. </div> </div> </div>Before that time in the WoD, as opposed to strict real world history, there still would have been prominent cities. The &quot;werewolves wiped out civilizations&quot; thing probably won't go away, much as how the world seems to have fewer cities with larger numbers, more old-growth wilderness, secret wolf packs, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other words, while the Red Talons would still be hostile to humans, I don't think they'd be particularly concerned about humans. I think they should be focusing on something else. Until 3000 BC or so, not even the great river valley agricultural civilizations are developed enough to worry many people. </div> </div> </div>They might have seen the worst of the alt-history human cities crop up bizarrely close to their territory. Maybe other monsters were aiding the humans. Their low opinion was likely formed early on, since the Talons don't really have any human ancestry of which to speak.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Today's Talons are really obsessed about humanity and their plight as a result of it. But that was not always the case. So in going back to these distant times, I'm wondering what other things were the Talons possibly interested in. Once we know that, then we can see if any of it may be of us for us running the tribe today. </div> </div> </div>It takes some effort for a shapeshifter to avoid one half of themselves for dozens or hundreds of millennia, and effort tends to require some kind of driving motivation.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T21:56:02-07:00","id":928591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=928511#post928511\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Before that time in the WoD, as opposed to strict real world history, there still would have been prominent cities. The &quot;werewolves wiped out civilizations&quot; thing probably won't go away, much as how the world seems to have fewer cities with larger numbers, more old-growth wilderness, secret wolf packs, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>What? This is just getting silly. Is this more nonsense from the Rokea book? I have no problem with an antedeluvian age in the World of Darkness where Conan or Atlantis or Hyperborea ruled and cities of an earlier version of Man existed, but that properly belongs in Mage or as backstory to Vampire. It doesn't work in Werewolf which is why I only include &quot;real time&quot; starting with the Holocene after the ice age ended.<br /> <br /> Anyway, this thread is now getting way past any value I hoped to talk about the Red Talons, and how they might be portrayed in a time when there were only 1-10 million people in the world living in Stone Age technology versus the modern world. There are discussions that lead to meaningful conversations, but this isn't one of them. It's just two people talking past each other. So now is time for beer.<br /> <br /> If I have any more ideas on the Red Talons, I'll still post them here though for your review and comments. Thanks for everyone's contributions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-09T07:45:50-07:00","id":928733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/912624-heretical-thoughts-on-griffin-and-the-red-talons?p=928591#post928591\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What? This is just getting silly. Is this more nonsense from the Rokea book? I have no problem with an antedeluvian age in the World of Darkness where Conan or Atlantis or Hyperborea ruled and cities of an earlier version of Man existed, but that properly belongs in Mage or as backstory to Vampire. It doesn't work in Werewolf which is why I only include &quot;real time&quot; starting with the Holocene after the ice age ended.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're reading it incorrectly. They weren't Atlantis or Hyperborea, just places that were slightly ahead of the curve with stonework and possibly running water (it's been a thing a lot earlier than people usually think). Because they were starting to mess with the landscape on a wider scale, the werewolves killed them. A few might have produced legends like the ones of Atlantis, but even cities that were aided along by early mages would probably not have been so grand as the embellished tales of later generations.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-12T04:43:29-07:00","id":930280,"title":"","content":"Well... technically the Dragon Kings did use to have an old antedeluvian age with grand kingdoms and stuff that would crumble... So there's precedent there <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-12T08:35:59-07:00","id":930359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l